<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tālivaldis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died 1215</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Talivaldis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tālivaldis (died 1215) was the ruler of Tālava, a Latgalian principality in north-eastern Latvia, known from the chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-livonia">Henry of Livonia</a>. Formerly a tributary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a>, he accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> baptism with his sons around 1208 and allied with the German crusaders against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a>. In 1215 Estonian raiders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sakala</a> captured him and roasted him to death.</p></div>
